From 38e6f038107154862cefbd92c3817ff3a967e672 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 15:13:47 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] common: match the printf conversion to the timeval
 field types

The timeval fields are printed with the long conversion throughout. That holds
on glibc, where suseconds_t is long, but not on Darwin, where time_t is 64 bit
and suseconds_t is 32 bit. Reading a 32 bit argument as a long takes whatever
sits beside it, so every microsecond field carries rubbish in its upper half.

In dci.csv the timestamp came out as 1791021297.4295804271, where the real
microsecond value is 4295804271 modulo 2^32, that is 836975. The statistics
file, the blind search timer and the stopwatch have the same defect.

Cast both fields to long at the call so the argument matches the conversion.
The cast is a no-op where the types are already long.

The copy in falcon_ue_dl.c.orig is left alone. It is not compiled.
---
 lib/src/phy/falcon_phch/falcon_dci.c | 8 ++++----
 lib/src/phy/falcon_ue/falcon_ue_dl.c | 6 +++---
 lib/src/prof/Stopwatch.cc            | 3 ++-
 src/src/PhyCommon.cc                 | 4 ++--
 src/src/SubframeInfoConsumer.cc      | 8 ++++----
 5 files changed, 15 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

